# Canonical implementation policy

This repository uses Git history as the rollback mechanism.
Do NOT preserve obsolete runtime code merely to make rollback possible.

## Canonical decisions

When a new implementation has been explicitly accepted as canonical:

1. Treat the canonical implementation as the only active source of truth.
2. Remove superseded experimental, transitional, placeholder, fallback UI,
   compatibility, and duplicate business-logic paths unless they are still
   required by a documented production compatibility constraint.
3. Do not keep old implementations commented out.
4. Do not keep dead components, unused helpers, obsolete locale keys,
   alternative rendering branches, or old feature behavior "just in case".
5. Do not reintroduce behavior from Git history unless the user explicitly
   requests restoration.

Git history is the archive. The runtime source tree is not an archive.

## Before adding a new implementation

Search for an existing canonical implementation first.

Prefer:
- extending the canonical implementation;
- replacing obsolete logic;
- deleting superseded logic;

over:
- creating parallel helpers;
- adding another fallback branch;
- preserving two competing implementations.

## Single source of truth

Business rules that affect multiple UI surfaces must have one canonical
derived model/helper.

Do not independently reproduce the same business-state interpretation in
multiple pages/components.

When duplicate implementations are found:
- determine which implementation is canonical;
- migrate callers to it;
- remove the obsolete implementation.

## Legacy code classification

Whenever modifying an existing subsystem, classify relevant old code as:

A. CANONICAL — keep and extend.
B. REQUIRED COMPATIBILITY — keep, document why and under what condition it
   may be removed.
C. TRANSITIONAL — remove once its migration/rollout condition is complete.
D. DEAD / SUPERSEDED — remove now.

Agents are authorized to delete C and D code as part of the task when
references and tests confirm it is no longer required.

Do not delete B code without proving that its compatibility requirement has
ended.

## UI experiments and placeholders

Temporary UI, mock payment states, coming-soon placeholders, experimental
flows, and transitional UX must not survive indefinitely after the real
feature becomes canonical.

Once the production implementation is accepted:
- remove the temporary runtime branch;
- remove its unused translations;
- remove its obsolete tests;
- replace them with regression tests for the canonical behavior.

## Feature flags

A feature flag is not permission to preserve obsolete implementations
forever.

After rollout is accepted and rollback risk is reasonably closed:
- remove the retired branch;
- remove the retired flag if no longer needed;
- preserve rollback capability through Git.

## Safety before deletion

Before deleting code:
1. search all references/imports;
2. inspect runtime entry points;
3. check tests;
4. check locale keys/config references;
5. run relevant tests and typecheck.

Deletion of obsolete code must not include unrelated cleanup.

## Agent reporting

When legacy code is encountered during a task, report:
- what was canonical;
- what was superseded;
- what was deleted;
- what compatibility code remains and why.

Do not preserve obsolete code solely because it may once have been useful.
